// @test: run
// @tier: 0
// @level: L2
// @tags: stdlib, io, fs, metadata, dispatch, mount, builtin-fallback
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-stdout: agree=true plausible=true
// @expected-exit: 0
//
// A METHOD CALL DOES NOT DEPEND ON WHETHER ITS TYPE IS NAMED IN THE MOUNT.
//
// `md.len()` on an `io.fs.Metadata` used to answer a small wrong number
// when the file was opened through the bare `mount core.io.fs;` form,
// and the right one when `Metadata` was also named in the mount. Same
// file, same call, same run — one mount line apart:
//
//     mount core.io.fs;                          md.len() -> 1
//     mount core.io.fs.{metadata, Metadata};     md.len() -> 10
//
// The receiver's type was not resolvable at the call site, so the call
// fell back to the builtin container-length opcode instead of
// dispatching to `Metadata.len`. There was no diagnostic: a wrong
// NUMBER, silently, on a call that looks correct.
//
// ONE IS THE WRONG ANSWER, AND THAT IS WHAT THIS SPEC WATCHES FOR.
// `Metadata` is `{ raw: FsMetadataRaw }` — exactly one field — so the
// builtin length of the record is 1. The two flags printed below are
// chosen so the fallback cannot produce them:
//
//     agree      the two mount spellings answer the same
//     plausible  the answer is not 1, the field count
//
// Under the defect `bare` was 1 and `named` was the real length, so
// BOTH flags flip. Under the fix both hold, on any platform, without
// pinning a number this spec would have to know in advance.
//
// IT STATS A PATH IT DOES NOT CREATE, deliberately, and IT LIVES IN A
// DIRECTORY OF ITS OWN. Both are consequences of the same thing: a spec
// is compiled together with its directory, so a broken neighbour fails
// it for a reason that is not its subject.
//
// Measured while writing this. The identical file PASSES here and FAILS
// one directory up, in `stdlib/io/`, with
//
//     Null pointer dereference: op=opcode 0x63 at OpenOptions.write
//
// which this spec never calls — `stdlib/io/file_write.vr` does, eight
// times. Four of the six specs in that directory are red today. An
// earlier version of this file also wrote its own fixture with
// `fs.write_str` and hit the same opcode, so the write is gone too.
//
// `/tmp` is present wherever this suite runs, and its length is not 1
// on any of them.
//
// The fix guards the fallback on a known non-container receiver. This
// spec pins the OUTCOME rather than the guard.
//
// WHY A SPEC AND NOT A UNIT TEST. The defect crosses the whole tier-0
// path — mount resolution, method dispatch, the VBC opcode choice — and
// its symptom is a plausible number rather than a crash. It also reached
// the documentation, which taught naming the type as a workaround.

mount core.io.fs;
mount core.io.fs.{metadata, Metadata};
mount core.io.path.{Path};
mount core.io.println;

fn main() {
    let path = "/tmp";

    let mut bare_len = -1;
    let mut named_len = -1;

    // THE SUBJECT: the receiver's type is not named in this call's mount
    // path. This is the spelling that answered 1.
    match fs.metadata(&fs.Path.from_str(&path)) {
        Result.Ok(md) => { bare_len = md.len(); }
        Result.Err(e) => {
            // ASSERT, do not merely print: a branch that prints and
            // returns leaves the exit code at 0, and `@expected-exit: 0`
            // would then pass a run that measured nothing.
            println(&"bare metadata failed");
            assert(false);
        }
    }

    // THE CONTROL: the same question with the type named. It was always
    // right, so a run where BOTH answers are wrong means the fixture or
    // the filesystem changed rather than the dispatch.
    match metadata(&Path.new(path)) {
        Result.Ok(md) => { named_len = md.len(); }
        Result.Err(e) => { println(&"named metadata failed"); assert(false); }
    }

    let agree = bare_len == named_len;
    let plausible = bare_len != 1;

    assert(agree);
    assert(plausible);
    println(&f"agree={agree} plausible={plausible}");
}
